<h2><span>Submission</span></h2>
<p><span>Submit one archive only:</span></p>
<pre><code><span>Lastname_Firstname_Project2.zip</span></code></pre>
<p><span>Select one of the three project options below. Submit files for on=
ly the option you selected.</span></p>
<h2><span>Overview</span></h2>
<p><span>Project 2 is a cumulative Linux systems investigation. It applies =
concepts studied during the first half of the semester, including:</span></=
p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><span>operating-system services and abstractions;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>user mode, kernel mode, and system calls;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code><span>fork()</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>exec()</span>=
</code><span>, and </span><code><span>wait()</span></code><span>;</span></p=
>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>system-call observation with </span><code><span>strace</span></cod=
e><span>;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>processes and POSIX threads;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>race conditions, critical sections, and mutexes;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>controlled performance experiments;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>repeated trials and raw CSV data;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>elapsed-time measurement with </span><code><span>clock_gettime()</=
span></code><span>;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>virtual memory and process memory regions;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code><span>/proc</span></code><span> memory information;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>memory allocation and page residency;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>pathnames, directories, dentries, and inodes;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>file descriptors and the Linux VFS;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>file metadata and permissions;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>page-cache behavior;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code><span>write()</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>read()</span=
></code><span>, and </span><code><span>fsync()</span></code><span>.</span><=
/p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><span>You will select one focused project track, run a controlled experi=
ment, preserve raw evidence, and write an evidence-based report.</span></p>
<h2><span>Project Options</span></h2>
<h3><span>Option 1 =E2=80=94 Synchronization and Performance</span></h3>
<p><span>Research question:</span></p>
<blockquote>
<p><span>How do worker count and mutex protection affect the correctness an=
d performance of a shared-counter workload?</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p><span>Use:</span></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><span>worker counts of 1, 2, and 4;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>a fixed total number of counter increments;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>an unsynchronized mode;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>a mutex-protected mode;</span></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><span>at least three trials for every condition.</span></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><span>This produces:</span></p>
<pre><code><span>3 worker counts =C3=97 2 synchronization modes =C3=97 3 tr=
ials
=3D 18 data rows</span></code></pre>
<p><span>Record:</span></p>
<ul>
